# Figure 6. Fleet selectivity compared with empirical capture-probability
# curves by hook size (coloured lines; Brulé et al. 2011; Christiansen et
# al. 2020), shown for the three hook fleets. Dotted lines give selectivity
# estimated freely from length compositions; solid lines give selectivity
# specified from the empirical curves for the hooks each fleet is documented
# to use, as adopted in the base configuration. The artisanal fleet provides
# internal validation: estimated and specified curves coincide, its peak
# matches the empirical curve for hook #12/0, and its selectivity at length
# at maturity (0.086) is consistent with the 95% immature fraction
# observed in pilot sampling. For the mid-range fleets the freely estimated
# curves peak 30 to 35 cm above the largest hook for which capture
# probability has been measured (#15/0, 47.3 cm) and above the mean length
# observed in pilot sampling (54.1 cm), and are asymptotic rather than
# domed; specifying them from the empirical curves improved the fit to
# length compositions by half and eliminated the parameter-bound problem
# that had affected 24 of 26 jitter replicates. Dashed vertical line marks
# length at 50 percent maturity (50.9 cm).
